# The same rules as PartCAD's own '.gitattributes' (partcad/partcad), which
# says at length why each of them is there. In short:
#
# Every text file is LF in the index and in the working tree, on every
# platform, whatever 'core.autocrlf' says locally. 'text=auto' sniffs a file's
# content before calling it text; every binary kind here is named below
# anyway, so what happens to it is declared rather than guessed.
#
# The STEP files are what every part is read from - build inputs - and so are
# kept in git itself, never in LFS: PartCAD fetches a package with a plain
# 'git clone', which fetches no LFS object, and a part read from a pointer file
# is a part that fails to build.
#
# What PartCAD renders - the drawings in 'examples/' that the README shows -
# is output that nothing reads back, rewritten in full whenever a model or the
# renderer changes. That is what LFS is for: kept in git, every revision of
# every drawing would stay in the history whole. A clone without LFS gets a
# pointer file there, which nothing here opens.
#
# Committing an image needs git-lfs installed and 'git lfs install' run in the
# clone. Without it git stores the file as it is, raw bytes at a path declared
# to hold a pointer, and says nothing (see AGENTS.md).
* text=auto eol=lf
*.step binary
*.stp binary
*.stl binary
*.3mf binary
*.pdf binary
**/*.jpg filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
**/*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
**/*.svg filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
**/*.pdf filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
